Rewrote the shadowed personal-tools menu in TypeScript rather than carrying Volto's file with a patch applied to it, and fixed what was worth fixing while it was open.

Three of them are real defects in the component as shipped. The Profile link's DOM id was `intl.formatMessage(messages.profile)` — the *translated* label, so `id="Profile"` in English and `id="Perfil"` in Portuguese, and anything keying on it works in one language and silently not in the next; it is `toolbar-profile` now, matching the `toolbar-logout` two lines below it. The back button and the logout link had no accessible name at all — an icon with a `title` inside an element with no label of its own is announced as an unnamed button — and now carry one, as the Preferences button beside them already did. And `const [, setPushed] = useState(false)` set a value nothing ever read, so its only effect was a re-render per menu push.

It also stops fetching the user. The menu decoded the session token and dispatched `getUser` on every open, for the same `@users/<userid>` this add-on already loads once per userid; that request is gone, and `jwt-decode` and `classnames` with it, which keeps the package free of runtime dependencies.

The component now has tests, which upstream ships none of: what differs from Volto is exactly what a future upgrade might quietly revert. `UserAvatar` gained a fallback for a portrait URL that fails to load — a deleted portrait rendered the browser's broken-image glyph, which reads as a bug in the site rather than as a missing photograph — and accepts a `className` from whatever places it. @ericof
